To follow up my own last e-mail to the list, this looks like it's useful
enough to share with the other alpha/redhat/linux novices out there.
Gurus please press 'd' :-)

OK, I just looked for strace on hotbot, and found 
http://www.gnome.org/gnomefaq/html/help.html

What is an strace? Someone asked me to send them one, how do I do this?

The strace command will run a program, outputting system calls and signals
as the program runs. This is a much more verbose debugging tool than the
backtrace, but sometimes you need the extra information an strace can give
you. Getting an strace is easy, just type 

  strace -o <output file> <command with args> 

into an xterm. For example, to do an strace on the gtalk program with sound
disabled, I might do strace -o /tmp/gtalk.strace gtalk --disable-sound.
This will save the strace output in /tmp/gtalk.strace. Strace does make use
of the path, and does accept program arguments on the command line. 

Warning, strace produces lots of output (the above strace example came up
with 5,498 lines in five seconds). Don't send straces to the mailing lists,
and only send them to the developers if they specifically request it. 


OK, I'm (partly) enlightened 

Cheerio those who made it to the bottom,
Phil


