Wes Bauske wrote:
> 
> I just received some DLT tapes from another company
> that were written on Sun Solaris with approximately
> 12MB physical block size. I tried to read them with
> Linux on my Alpha but Linux fails with the messge
> 
> "dd: /dev/st0: Value too large for defined data type"
> 
> dmesg shows:
> 
> st: failed to enlarge buffer to 11987040 bytes.
> 
> It seems Linux is running into a limit on the order of 2MB
> from my other testing.
> 
> I looked a bit at the code and I think a kmalloc()
> for that size failed, or, possibly it tried to get a
> DMA region that large. Anyone have any ideas how to
> get the tape read?
> 

The problem is in mm/page_alloc.c

A define for NR_MEM_LISTS with a value of 6 is too small
to allocate sufficient memory for the st device driver.
All you have to do is set NR_MEM_LISTS to 7 or more. I set
it to 8 and rebuilt my kernel and it works like a champ
so far. I'm a gigabyte into the tape and it's still 
chunking along. I believe I can now go to 32MB blocks
assuming I understand how it all works.

Anyone know if setting this define larger causes trouble
anywhere else?? It appears it controls the number and allowed 
size of the memory pool chunks. By increasing it from 6 to 8
I'm allowing chunks 2x and 4x larger than the previous max.
The term used is the "order" of the chunk which seems to be
just the size requested rounded to a page size times a power
of 2.

Another alternative is to change the number of scatter/gather
buffers in st.c to allow more than 16 segments. Either way 
allows st.c to allocate sufficient space to do the large tape
blocks. Perhaps modifying st.c is preferable to mucking with
page_alloc.c assuming there is no issue with using more than
16 s/g segments.


Wes


